{
  "title": "for, range, enumerate, zip",
  "summary": "Loop over things instead of over indexes.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "for loops over things",
      "body": [
        "Python's `for` is C#'s `foreach`. There is no `for (int i = 0; ...)` form:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "for ch in \"abc\":",
        "    print(ch)",
        "for name in [\"Ann\", \"Bo\"]:",
        "    print(name)",
        "```",
        "",
        "When you do need numbers, `range` produces them:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "range(5)         # 0 1 2 3 4",
        "range(2, 6)      # 2 3 4 5       (stop excluded)",
        "range(10, 0, -3) # 10 7 4 1",
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "match",
      "prompt": "Match each range to the numbers it produces.",
      "pairs": [
        [
          "`range(4)`",
          "0 1 2 3"
        ],
        [
          "`range(1, 4)`",
          "1 2 3"
        ],
        [
          "`range(0, 10, 4)`",
          "0 4 8"
        ],
        [
          "`range(3, 0, -1)`",
          "3 2 1"
        ],
        [
          "`range(5, 5)`",
          "(nothing)"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "total = 0",
        "for i in range(1, 11, 2):",
        "    total += i",
        "print(total)"
      ],
      "answer": "25",
      "explain": "1 + 3 + 5 + 7 + 9 = 25. The stop value 11 is never produced."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "enumerate and zip",
      "body": [
        "Do not write `for i in range(len(items))`. Ask for what you need:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "names = [\"Ann\", \"Bo\", \"Cy\"]",
        "scores = [90, 75, 82]",
        "",
        "for i, name in enumerate(names, start=1):   # index + item",
        "    print(i, name)",
        "",
        "for name, score in zip(names, scores):      # walk two lists together",
        "    print(f\"{name}: {score}\")",
        "```",
        "",
        "`zip` stops at the shortest input. Both are like LINQ's `Select((x, i) => ...)` and `Zip`, built in."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "Which loop is the idiomatic way to print each item with its 0-based position?",
      "options": [
        "`for i, x in enumerate(items):`",
        "`for i in range(len(items)): x = items[i]`",
        "`for (i, x) in items.index():`",
        "`for x in items: i = items.index(x)`"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "`enumerate` is clearer and faster. The last one is also wrong when items repeat, because `index` finds the first match."
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "for i, (a, b) in enumerate(zip(\"xyz\", [10, 20])):",
        "    print(i, a, b)"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "0 x 10",
        "1 y 20"
      ],
      "explain": "`zip` stops when the shorter input (two numbers) runs out, so `z` is never reached."
    },
    {
      "type": "blanks",
      "prompt": "Print `1. tea`, `2. milk` and so on.",
      "code": [
        "items = [\"tea\", \"milk\", \"eggs\"]",
        "for __1__, item in __2__(items, start=__3__):",
        "    print(f\"{n}. {item}\")"
      ],
      "blanks": [
        "n",
        "enumerate",
        "1"
      ],
      "bank": [
        "zip",
        "0",
        "range",
        "i"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Times table",
      "prompt": [
        "Read `n`. Print the `n` times table from 1 to 10, with the products right-aligned in a field of 3:",
        "",
        "```text",
        " 7 x  1 =   7",
        " 7 x  2 =  14",
        "...",
        " 7 x 10 =  70",
        "```",
        "",
        "Each line is `f\"{n:>2} x {i:>2} = {n * i:>3}\"`."
      ],
      "starter": "n = int(input())\n",
      "solution": [
        "n = int(input())",
        "for i in range(1, 11):",
        "    print(f\"{n:>2} x {i:>2} = {n * i:>3}\")"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "name": "7",
          "stdin": "7\n",
          "expect": [
            " 7 x  1 =   7",
            " 7 x  2 =  14",
            " 7 x  3 =  21",
            " 7 x  4 =  28",
            " 7 x  5 =  35",
            " 7 x  6 =  42",
            " 7 x  7 =  49",
            " 7 x  8 =  56",
            " 7 x  9 =  63",
            " 7 x 10 =  70"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "12",
          "stdin": "12\n",
          "expect": [
            "12 x  1 =  12",
            "12 x  2 =  24",
            "12 x  3 =  36",
            "12 x  4 =  48",
            "12 x  5 =  60",
            "12 x  6 =  72",
            "12 x  7 =  84",
            "12 x  8 =  96",
            "12 x  9 = 108",
            "12 x 10 = 120"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "require": [
        {
          "pattern": "\\bfor\\b.*\\brange\\(",
          "message": "Uses a for loop over range"
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`range(1, 11)` gives 1 to 10."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Report card",
      "prompt": [
        "Read a line of names separated by spaces, then a line of scores separated by spaces (same count). Print each student numbered from 1, then the best:",
        "",
        "```text",
        "1. Ann 90",
        "2. Bo 75",
        "best: Ann",
        "```",
        "",
        "If two share the best score, the first one wins."
      ],
      "starter": "names = input().split()\nscores = input().split()\n",
      "solution": [
        "names = input().split()",
        "scores = [int(s) for s in input().split()]",
        "best_name, best = None, None",
        "for i, (name, score) in enumerate(zip(names, scores), start=1):",
        "    print(f\"{i}. {name} {score}\")",
        "    if best is None or score > best:",
        "        best_name, best = name, score",
        "print(f\"best: {best_name}\")"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "name": "two students",
          "stdin": "Ann Bo\n90 75\n",
          "expect": [
            "1. Ann 90",
            "2. Bo 75",
            "best: Ann"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "tie goes to first",
          "stdin": "Cy Di Ed\n60 88 88\n",
          "expect": [
            "1. Cy 60",
            "2. Di 88",
            "3. Ed 88",
            "best: Di"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "require": [
        {
          "pattern": "\\benumerate\\(",
          "message": "Uses enumerate"
        },
        {
          "pattern": "\\bzip\\(",
          "message": "Uses zip"
        }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "`for i, (name, score) in enumerate(zip(names, scores), start=1):`",
        "Convert each score with `int(...)` before comparing.",
        "Only replace the best when the new score is strictly greater."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
